I chose candidate 6. I estimated this by reasoning only. I didn't run a step-by-step simulation, so this is an approximate judgement.

1. **Cost per order day (47).** Ordering every day (candidates 0 and 2) costs about 26 × 47 ≈ 1,222. Ordering every second day costs about 611. Every third day (candidate 5) costs about 423, and every fourth day (candidate 3) about 329.
2. **Storage cost (0.8 per unit per day).** Bigger batches mean more stock left on the shelf each evening. Going from daily to every-other-day orders adds about 30 units of average stock, which is roughly 24 per day or about 630 over the month. That almost exactly cancels the ~611 saved on order fees, so daily and every-other-day ordering come out about even. Going to every third day adds about another 720 in storage, more than the 188 it saves in fees, so candidate 5 loses.
3. **Warehouse limit of 216 units.** Candidate 3 orders every four days, so its batches overflow the warehouse. Around day 12, about 178 units of product 0 arrive while roughly 74 units of product 1 are still in stock. The excess is lost even though it was already paid for. Candidate 5 is close to the limit around day 22 and sometimes exceeds it when demand is random.
4. **How much extra stock to order.** Average real demand is about 1.046 × the forecast. A missed sale costs about 13 (8 of lost margin plus a 5 penalty). An unsold unit costs about 5 plus storage, and unsold stock carries over until it expires, so it is not lost right away. That favours ordering a bit above average demand. Each every-other-day order covers two days of demand, which smooths out the randomness (spread of about 21% instead of 30% for one day). In that setting, about 1.15 × the forecast is a sensible level:
   - candidate 1 orders exactly the forecast, so it runs short too often;
   - candidate 4 orders about 1.10 × the forecast, a thin margin;
   - candidate 6 orders about 1.14–1.16 × the forecast, which fits best;
   - candidate 7 orders about 1.25 × the forecast, so extra stock builds up, costs storage and expires.

   Among the daily-order plans, candidate 0 (about 1.1×) and candidate 2 (about 1.2×) end up roughly level with the every-other-day plans after fees and storage. Their buffer is less well matched, because a single day's demand varies more.
